INT. DAGUERREOTYPE STUDIO - DAY - 1843

A top-floor room in Boston. Skylight, white drapes, a posing chair with a headrest clamp. A wooden camera on a tripod. Silver plates in a rack. The smell of mercury and iodine.

ELIJAH THORNBURY, 40s, in a stained frock coat, adjusts the camera with practiced fingers. MISS CORNELIA PEMBERLEY, 30s, in a black mourning dress and bonnet, stands in the doorway holding a small wrapped bundle.

ELIJAH
Come in, miss.

CORNELIA
Thank you.

ELIJAH
You are the lady who wrote.

CORNELIA
I am.

ELIJAH
You said it was a portrait of a child.

She enters. Her step is careful. She sets the bundle on the table beside the plates, and unfolds the cloth. A small girl, five years old, in a white christening gown, asleep.

Elijah goes quiet. He wipes his hands on his coat.

ELIJAH (CONT'D)
When?

CORNELIA
Sunday.

ELIJAH
I am sorry.

CORNELIA
She never had a likeness made. I thought, if I could just. If you could.

ELIJAH
I have done it before. It is not an easy thing.

CORNELIA
I will pay double.

ELIJAH
Miss Pemberley. I will not take your money.

She nods. Her lips shake. She straightens the child's gown.

CORNELIA
Where shall I put her?

Elijah brings the posing chair into the light. He tilts the headrest. He kneels, and with great delicacy folds the little hands across the gown.

ELIJAH
Here. Where the sun can find her.

Cornelia stands beside him. He steps to the camera. Slides in the plate. She watches him without breathing.

ELIJAH (CONT'D)
You must be very still, Miss Pemberley.

CORNELIA
I will.

ELIJAH
Even your breath.

CORNELIA
I will not breathe.

He removes the lens cap. They hold, the three of them, in the white light. The seconds pass.
